import {ToolRegistry} from './registry.js';

describe('ToolRegistry.execute', () => {
  it('returns a ToolResponse naming the missing required field and does not invoke the executor', async () => {
    const registry = new ToolRegistry();
    let executorCalls = 0;
    registry.register(
      {
        name: 'lookup_address',
        description: 'Look up an address by postal code',
        inputSchema: {
          type: 'object',
          properties: {postalCode: {type: 'string'}},
          required: ['postalCode'],
        },
      },
      async () => {
        executorCalls += 1;
        return 'sunny';
      }
    );

    const response = await registry.execute('lookup_address', {});

    expect({executorCalls, result: response.result}).toEqual({
      executorCalls: 0,
      result: expect.stringMatching(/postalCode.*required|required.*postalCode/i),
    });
  });

  it('reports a nested missing required property with its full field path', async () => {
    const registry = new ToolRegistry();
    registry.register(
      {
        name: 'lookup_address',
        description: 'Look up an address by country',
        inputSchema: {
          type: 'object',
          properties: {
            address: {
              type: 'object',
              properties: {country: {type: 'string'}},
              required: ['country'],
            },
          },
          required: ['address'],
        },
      },
      async () => 'unreachable'
    );

    const response = await registry.execute('lookup_address', {address: {}});

    expect(response.result).toMatch(/\/address\/country.*required/i);
  });

  it('throws at register time when the inputSchema is uncompilable and the message names the tool', () => {
    const registry = new ToolRegistry();

    expect(() =>
      registry.register(
        {
          name: 'broken_weather',
          description: 'Has an invalid JSON Schema type',
          inputSchema: {
            type: 'object',
            properties: {forecast: {type: 'not-a-json-schema-type'}},
          },
        },
        async () => 'unreachable'
      )
    ).toThrow(/broken_weather/);
  });

  it('rejects an enum value with its field path and allowed values', async () => {
    const registry = new ToolRegistry();
    registry.register(
      {
        name: 'get_temperature',
        description: 'Get a temperature in a supported unit',
        inputSchema: {
          type: 'object',
          properties: {unit: {enum: ['celsius', 'fahrenheit']}},
          required: ['unit'],
        },
      },
      async () => 'unreachable'
    );

    const response = await registry.execute('get_temperature', {unit: 'kelvin'});

    expect(response.result).toMatch(/\/unit.*celsius.*fahrenheit/i);
  });
});

--- validation spec ---
# Tool Input Validation

| Field  | Value                |
| ------ | -------------------- |
| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
| Status | In Progress          |

Every tool registered in `src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts` declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` that is forwarded to the configured AI provider, and `ToolRegistry.execute` validates the model-supplied `input` against that schema before calling the tool's executor. When validation fails, `execute` returns a rejection `ToolResponse` describing what was wrong rather than throwing, so the orchestrator feeds the failure back to the model as a `tool_result` within the existing tool-round budget.

## Validation at execute time

- When `input` omits a property listed in `required`, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the missing property and its full path as required and never invokes the executor ([validated by: returns a ToolResponse naming the missing required field and does not invoke the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L4), [validated by: reports a nested missing required property with its full field path](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L31), [validated by: rejects input missing a required property with a ToolResponse and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L16)).
- When `input` supplies a property whose value does not match its declared JSON Schema type, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` naming the failing field path and expected type without invoking the executor, and neither `result` nor the emitted log record contains the offending property value ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property naming the field and expected type without leaking the offending value to the result or logs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L45)).
- When `input` satisfies `required`, declared property types and `enum`, `ToolRegistry.execute` invokes the executor once and passes the input object through unchanged — including undeclared extra properties — without injecting `additionalProperties: false`, coercing types or applying defaults. An input that violates an `enum` is rejected with the failing field path and allowed values ([validated by: rejects an enum value with its field path and allowed values](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L75)).
- When `ToolRegistry.execute` is called with a tool name that has not been registered, it throws `Unknown tool: <name>` because an unregistered tool is a programming or configuration error rather than a recoverable input mistake.

## Schema compilation and caching

- A static `ToolDefinition` has its `inputSchema` compiled once at `register` time, so a schema that is not valid JSON Schema throws synchronously from `register` with an error message naming the offending tool ([validated by: throws at register time when the inputSchema is uncompilable and the message names the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L57), [validated by: throws at register time naming the tool when a static inputSchema cannot be compiled](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L93)).
- A static tool's `inputSchema` is compiled at `register` time and reused on every `execute` call without re-reading or re-compiling `definition.inputSchema`.
- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` resolves its definition on each `execute` call and validates against the `inputSchema` returned for that call, caching compiled validators by schema object reference (`WeakMap`) so distinct returned schemas each enforce their own rules.

## Orchestrator tool-loop recovery

- Because a schema rejection returns a `ToolResponse` instead of throwing, `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts` sends the rejection `result` back to the provider as a `tool_result` message, allowing the model to issue a corrected tool call on the next round and complete the turn within `maxToolRounds` ([validated by: returns a schema rejection as a tool_result in the orchestrator loop so the model can correct the call on the next round](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L111)).

